WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.232
Names often contain formatting differences that make analysis and matching harder.

00:00:05.232 --> 00:00:09.747
The query starts by trimming spaces at the beginning and end of the name.

00:00:09.747 --> 00:00:17.072
It then uses a regular expression replacement to collapse repeated internal spaces into a single space.

00:00:17.072 --> 00:00:21.147
Finally, it applies consistent display capitalization.

00:00:21.608 --> 00:00:29.434
For example, imagine the source value contains several spaces before Jane, two spaces between Jane and Smith,

00:00:29.434 --> 00:00:34.519
and several spaces afterward. The cleaned representation becomes simply Jane Smith.

00:00:34.519 --> 00:00:36.074
Notice what we are not doing.

00:00:36.074 --> 00:00:40.764
We are not deciding whether Jane Smith and Jane A.

00:00:40.764 --> 00:00:43.494
Smith are the same person.

00:00:43.494 --> 00:00:45.644
That would be an identity question.

00:00:45.644 --> 00:00:49.519
Here we are only normalizing spacing and display case.

00:00:49.519 --> 00:00:56.033
Run the query and compare the raw name with the cleaned name so you can see exactly what changed.
